INFO:agent.loop:\u26a1 Executing tool: 'final_report' with arguments: {"investigation_summary": "Target: Conti ransomware leaks and public attribution record. Use only benchmark sources SRC-001 through SRC-004 and preserve source IDs. SRC-001 (Rewards for Justice) states Conti is also known as Wizard Spider and is a Russian government-linked ransomware-as-a-service group targeting U.S. and Western critical infrastructure. It says online aliases Target, Reshaev, Professor, Tramp, and Dandis are believed linked to Conti; associated location Russia; associated malicious applications Conti ransomware-as-a-service, TrickBot, IcedID, Cobalt Strike, and ZLoader. It reports first detection in 2019, over 1,000 ransomware operations, more than 400 organizations worldwide victimized including over 290 in the U.S.; theft plus encryption; ransom portal; stolen data sold or published if unpaid; demands up to $25 million; and a post-February-2022 invasion pledge of support for Russia and threats against infrastructure of countries perceived to be at war with Russia. SRC-002 (Rapid7) states @ContiLeaks released 14MB of chat logs in 393 JSON files on 2022-02-27, with some encrypted files, so incomplete. It reports internal communications, tools, exploit/design discussions, credentials, alleged server access and alleged source code; says the leaker was likely a senior member or coalition of disgruntled affiliates. The chats indicate hiring/interviews, coding exercises, salary negotiations, products, a primary Bitcoin address reportedly holding over $2B on 2022-02-28, and a conversation allegedly indicating Russian FSB involvement in some funding and interest in Bellingcat/Navalny files. Rapid7 also describes software-company-like development, coders/operators, testers, phishing, control panel, third-party malicious documents, and that Conti took down exposed infrastructure then resumed normal operations by 2022-03-09. These are Rapid7 assessments/reports and some are allegations; do not present them as adjudicated facts. SRC-003 (Center for Internet Security) calls Conti a notorious RaaS using double extortion and says it appears tied to TrickBot. It explains RaaS as developers leasing malware to affiliates who conduct intrusions and share profits, often 70/30; initial access brokers can sell access. It reports Conti business units and specialized roles: Coders, Testers, Administrators, Reverse Engineers, Penetration Testers/Hackers; spamming contracted outside; separate budgets and ability to borrow; an insider claimed more than 60 employees in July 2021. It reports budgets for Cobalt Strike and security/AV testing, and that Conti platform shut down/rebranded into smaller units in May 2022, with leak and negotiation infrastructure shut down about a month later. SRC-004 (DOJ Middle District of Tennessee) lists case 3:23-CR-00092 with defendants Maksim Galochkin, Maksim Rudenskiy, Mikhail Tsarev, Andrey Zhuykov, charged with conspiracy to violate CFAA and conspiracy to commit wire fraud; and case 3:23-CR-00088 with Oleksii Lytvynenko, charged with conspiracy to deploy Conti. It says Conti affected more than 900 victims worldwide; defendants are presumed innocent. It reports on 2026-06-10 that Lytvynenko pleaded guilty to Count 2, conspiracy to commit wire fraud, with sentencing scheduled 2026-09-10. No benchmark source links any named defendant to one of the five Rewards-for-Justice aliases, and no source establishes that the aliases are legal identities. The KB contains entities and supported relations: ALIAS_FOR Wizard Spider/Target/Reshaev/Professor/Tramp/Dandis -> Conti; Conti AFFILIATED_WITH Russia; Conti ASSOCIATED_WITH_MALWARE_ECOSYSTEM Conti ransomware-as-a-service, TrickBot, IcedID, Cobalt Strike, ZLoader; ContiLeaks chat archive PUBLISHED_BY ContiLeaks and LINKED_TO Rapid7; event timing; DOJ cases published by DOJ; named persons CHARGED_IN_RELATED_CONSPIRACY to the two cases; Lytvynenko associated with 2026-06-10. Need a readable Markdown final report plus fenced JSON arrays findings, claims, reasoning_steps, contradictions. Cite only SRC-001..SRC-004. Include uncertainty and contradictions: incomplete encrypted leak archive; Rapid7 allegation of FSB funding; RFJ says >1,000 operations while DOJ says >900 victims (different metrics, not necessarily contradiction); platform shutdown/rebranding vs Rapid7 observed resumed operations (different dates/operational layers)."}
